</p></notes></front><body id="jgc42020-body-0001"><p>Bennett, R. L., French, K. S., Resta, R. G., &amp; Austin, J. (2022). Practice resource‐focused revision: Standardized pedigree nomenclature update centered on sex and gender inclusivity: A practice resource of the National Society of Genetic Counselors. <italic toggle="yes">Journal of Genetic Counseling</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">31</italic>(6), 1238–1248. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="https://doi.org/10.1002/jgc4.1621" ext-link-type="uri">https://doi.org/10.1002/jgc4.1621</ext-link>.</p><p>As per Rathbun and Paulyson Nuñez (Rathbun &amp; Paulyson Nuñez, <xref rid="jgc42020-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">in press</xref>) and our Reply to Rathbun and Paulyson Nuñez (French et al., <xref rid="jgc42020-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">in press</xref>), we inaccurately and interchangeably used the terms “surrogate” and “gestational carrier” in our previous publication (Bennett et al., <xref rid="jgc42020-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">2022</xref> Figure 5 examples 3 and 4). A “gestational carrier” is defined as a person who carries a pregnancy resulting from the transfer of a preimplantation embryo, but who has no genetic relationship with the embryo. The term “surrogate” is defined as a person who donates eggs <italic toggle="yes">and</italic> carries the pregnancy for another. We have updated our recommendations accordingly (see Figure 5).</p><p>We now suggest removing the symbol for surrogate “S” (Bennett et al., <xref rid="jgc42020-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">2022</xref>, Figure 5 example 3) and instead using the gestational carrier symbol “G.” We also recommend adding a “G” to the circle on the right to indicate a gestational carrier in the 2022 article, Figure 5, example 5 (see corrected Figure 5, example 4). This can clarify some reproductively complex pedigrees. In addition, regarding Figure 5 example 5 from our 2022 article, we now recommend removing the “D” from the circle on the left, since this is an intended parent and not an egg donor.</p><p>In reviewing Figure 4 from our 2022 publication, we noted that the symbol for ectopic pregnancy was inconsistent with the 2008 guidelines (Bennett et al., <xref rid="jgc42020-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">2008</xref>, Figure 1); the forward slash was accidentally omitted. Our corrected Figure 4 reflects this change.</p><p>In our Correction, we amended the Figures to reflect the above changes and made some minor revisions to improve clarity (Figures <xref rid="jgc42020-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>, <xref rid="jgc42020-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2</xref>, <xref rid="jgc42020-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3</xref>, <xref rid="jgc42020-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4</xref>, <xref rid="jgc42020-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5</xref>).</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="jgc42020-fig-0001" orientation="portrait"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>Sex and gender in pedigree nomenclature.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-1" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="JGC4-34-0-g003.jpg"><?image-name JGC4-34-0-g003.jpg?><?image-size 167122?><?image-md5 877c6c8d27096036a9959bc265682ac2?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 1190?><?image-original-width 1064?><?image-scaled-height 793?><?image-scaled-width 709?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/df46/11926493/877c6c8d2709/JGC4-34-0-g003.jpg?><?thumb-name JGC4-34-0-g003.gif?><?thumb-size 4600?><?thumb-md5 2f81128abb645dbf67f829f6d6a5afcf?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 112?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/df46/11926493/2f81128abb64/JGC4-34-0-g003.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="jgc42020-fig-0002" orientation="portrait"><label>FIGURE 2</label><caption><p>Common pedigree definitions, symbols, and abbreviations.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-3" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="JGC4-34-0-g001.jpg"><?image-name JGC4-34-0-g001.jpg?><?image-size 178466?><?image-md5 0ae5a38353bf2d17c0eb100e4becb074?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 1909?><?image-original-width 1064?><?image-scaled-height 1272?><?image-scaled-width 709?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/df46/11926493/0ae5a38353bf/JGC4-34-0-g001.jpg?><?thumb-name JGC4-34-0-g001.gif?><?thumb-size 5742?><?thumb-md5 1881027fedd2e3bc3c413828ca9f95de?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 179?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/df46/11926493/1881027fedd2/JGC4-34-0-g001.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="jgc42020-fig-0003" orientation="portrait"><label>FIGURE 3</label><caption><p>Pedigree line definitions.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-5" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="JGC4-34-0-g002.jpg"><?image-name JGC4-34-0-g002.jpg?><?image-size 131047?><?image-md5 ee6ad7a0fcc1106dd7f2c4375e5be84f?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 1358?><?image-original-width 1064?><?image-scaled-height 905?><?image-scaled-width 709?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/df46/11926493/ee6ad7a0fcc1/JGC4-34-0-g002.jpg?><?thumb-name JGC4-34-0-g002.gif?><?thumb-size 4288?><?thumb-md5 f0f94a37329c306a37ccc643e206874d?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 128?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/df46/11926493/f0f94a37329c/JGC4-34-0-g002.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="jgc42020-fig-0004" orientation="portrait"><label>FIGURE 4</label><caption><p>Pedigree symbols related to pregnancy, miscarriage, and termination of pregnancy.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-7" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="JGC4-34-0-g005.jpg"><?image-name JGC4-34-0-g005.jpg?><?image-size 138269?><?image-md5 7f3e5b2c7ca87637ebec539b26d13911?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 1821?><?image-original-width 1064?><?image-scaled-height 1213?><?image-scaled-width 709?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/df46/11926493/7f3e5b2c7ca8/JGC4-34-0-g005.jpg?><?thumb-name JGC4-34-0-g005.gif?><?thumb-size 4600?><?thumb-md5 b0d911aa9987f344a4e43578647f51fc?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 171?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/df46/11926493/b0d911aa9987/JGC4-34-0-g005.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="jgc42020-fig-0005" orientation="portrait"><label>FIGURE 5</label><caption><p>Assisted reproduction symbols.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-9" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="JGC4-34-0-g004.jpg"><?image-name JGC4-34-0-g004.jpg?><?image-size 208811?><?image-md5 a7a6908745886a4cf1abbb929818b346?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 2172?><?image-original-width 1064?><?image-scaled-height 1447?><?image-scaled-width 709?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/df46/11926493/a7a690874588/JGC4-34-0-g004.jpg?><?thumb-name JGC4-34-0-g004.gif?><?thumb-size 5942?><?thumb-md5 beb5d9ea7fc07a0107f7f4a9f61395ba?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 204?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/df46/11926493/beb5d9ea7fc0/JGC4-34-0-g004.gif?></graphic></fig><p>In recognition of the continual evolution of language regarding reproduction and gender, we recommend regular review of this standardized pedigree nomenclature to align with rapidly changing definitions of reproduction and gender.</p><sec id="jgc42020-sec-0526"><title>AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS</title><p>All authors contributed equally to the conception, writing and graphic design.</p></sec></body><back><sec sec-type="data-availability" id=